  <page_number>176</page_number>
  <running_head>SAM. PETITI COMMENTARIVS</running_head>
  <bottom_margin>
  </bottom_margin>
  <main_text>
    <p cont="true">rum clientelam vltro venerunt, atque illis operas suas locarunt. Refert Posidonius Stoicus Historia X. &amp; verba transcripsit Athenaeus in Lib. VI. <illegible, ~150 letters> Qua sola vera atque multa erat seruitus, vt quæ à natura esset: vnde Aristoteles: <illegible, ~120 letters> Anima enim illis pro tali data est. Idem maximus Doctor: <illegible, ~90 letters> &amp; alterius natura est, id est, seruus est, qui suus esse non potest: <illegible, ~60 letters> Praeterca qui Lege serui sunt, <illegible, ~40 letters>, inquit summus virorum, <illegible, ~60 letters> liberalitate potius quàm metu retinendi sunt</p>
    <p><i><illegible, ~200 letters></i></p>
    <p>inquit Anaxandrides Comicus in Anchise. Ad haec metus erat, ne prouocati iniuriis Serui bello dominos lacesserent, quod aliquando euenisse testatur reliquit Posidonius Philosophus: tantusenim Athenis erat Seruorum numerus, vt quum Demetrius Phalereus anno primo Olympiadis centesimæ decimæ censum Athenienstium ageret, numerata sint seruorum capitum quadringenta millia. Anonymus in Descriptione Olympiadum ad hunc annum: <illegible, ~110 letters> Crescicles Lib. ro III. Chronicorum idem scribit: <illegible, ~190 letters> Humaneitaque Seruos suos habuerunt Athenienses.</p>
    <p>Hinc Legem vt scriberent hanc, factum est:</p>
    <p><illegible, ~60 letters> Si quis alienum seruum pulsarit, aduersus eum Lege iniuriarum agito.</p>
    <p>Non licebat igitur alienum seruum pulsare: meminit huius Legis Xenophon <illegible, ~170 letters> Hanc eandem Legem intelligit Athenaeus Libro VI. <illegible, ~190 letters> Plato vocat, quae proprie αἰκία est, id est, πληγαὶ μόνον, vt interpretantur veteres Magistri, cuius multa erat pecuniaria, vt docet Lysias <illegible, ~120 letters></p>
  </main_text>
  <notes>Most of the Greek is too faint to read letter by letter at this scan resolution, so each Greek line is marked illegible with an approximate letter count rather than guessed. The Latin is legible except where marked [?]. The Greek verse stanza is shown as one illegible block. The mark at the lower right below the last line is not readable and is not transcribed as a catchword or signature. "Lib. ro III." is copied as printed and may be a misreading. "Humaneitaque" is copied as printed and may be a misreading.</notes>
</page>